From 9d2b3c4d5e6f70819a0b1c2d3e4f5a6b7c8d9e0f1a2b3c4d5e6f70819a0b1d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: noname <noname@example.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 18:20:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 0046 name the colour description the stream carries, once a session

The seat's picture looked wrong on the owner's television - dark colours reading
darker than the same numbers on a PC monitor and reds reading further into red -
and the question was which link is at fault: the stream, the decoder, the seat's
own colour handling, or the display. Nothing in the series had ever measured the
colour description the stream carries, because nothing prints it: the seat's
decode side models it fully (`StreamFormat` holds the pixel format, the range,
the matrix, the primaries, the transfer function and the chroma siting) and the
seat's handoff to a compositor drops it, because the legacy DMA-BUF interface
describes planes and nothing else - `zwp_linux_buffer_params_v1_add` has no room
for primaries and no room for a transfer function, so a display downstream can
only guess them. Guessing is exactly the shape of the complaint: a display that
applies its own native transfer function and a wider primary set to numbers made
as BT.709 with the video range pushes saturation outward and deepens everything
from black to mid-grey at once, which is what a set with a wide default colour
space does to an untagged 8-bit signal.

`0046` prints the description the stream declared, once per session, on both
paths a decoded frame takes: the DMA-BUF handoff (printed beside `0043`'s own
band line, since that is where the description is dropped) and the CPU copy,
whose existing diagnostic line named only the range, the matrix and the chroma.
The new line reads `opennow-diag: stream colour: pixels=... range=... matrix=...
primaries=... transfer=... chroma=...` with every field's debug name, so the
gamut a session carries (BT.709 or the wider BT.2020) and whether the coded
values follow the video EOTF or an sRGB one can be read from the log next to
what the compositor decided for its own output (`using color profile:` and
`attempts EOTF mode:`), and the two sides can be compared rather than argued
about.

Nothing is converted and no colour is touched: the patch adds two print calls to
paths that already run once per session, so an unchanged session behaves exactly
as it did, and the cost when the log is discarded is a branch on an already
swapped atomic.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [STM32MP25 performance work]

--- a/native/opennow-streamer/crates/opennow-streamer-platform-linux/src/frame_producer.rs
+++ b/native/opennow-streamer/crates/opennow-streamer-platform-linux/src/frame_producer.rs
@@ -527,6 +527,28 @@
         "opennow-diag: dma-buf export fd band: duplicates start at {}",
         crate::EXPORT_FD_BAND,
     ));
+    // Says once what the stream claims about the colours it carries, so the
+    // numbers a seat sends can be read against what the display downstream
+    // assumes: the legacy DMA-BUF handoff has no room for primaries or a
+    // transfer function (`zwp_linux_buffer_params_v1_add` describes planes,
+    // nothing else), so the description the bitstream's own header carried is
+    // dropped right here and a display has to guess it. The range names the
+    // video convention (`Limited` is luma 16..241 with chroma 33..223, which is
+    // what a hantro decode of a limited-range picture reports), the matrix
+    // folds YUV into RGB, the primaries name the gamut the container declared,
+    // the transfer function says whether the coded values follow the video EOTF
+    // or an sRGB one, and the pixel format says whether the picture is 8-bit
+    // SDR at all.
+    static COLOUR_LOGGED: std::sync::Once = std::sync::Once::new();
+    COLOUR_LOGGED.call_once(|| eprintln!(
+        "opennow-diag: stream colour: pixels={:?} range={:?} matrix={:?} primaries={:?} transfer={:?} chroma={:?}",
+        frame.format.pixel_format,
+        frame.format.color_range,
+        frame.format.color_matrix,
+        frame.format.color_primaries,
+        frame.format.color_transfer,
+        frame.format.chroma_location,
+    ));
     // The buffer is described at the visible height even when the decoder pads
     // the picture (1088 rows for 1080p): the planes carry their own offsets, so
     // the extra rows never reach the compositor, and a surface matching the
@@ -1286,6 +1308,15 @@
                     frame.format.color_matrix,
                     frame.format.chroma_location
                 );
+                eprintln!(
+                    "opennow-diag: stream colour: pixels={:?} range={:?} matrix={:?} primaries={:?} transfer={:?} chroma={:?}",
+                    frame.format.pixel_format,
+                    frame.format.color_range,
+                    frame.format.color_matrix,
+                    frame.format.color_primaries,
+                    frame.format.color_transfer,
+                    frame.format.chroma_location
+                );
             }
         }
         let planes = frame
